  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 311 AM EDT Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A picture perfect start the weekend looks to be in store \nbeginning today through Saturday. Temperatures will trend slightly \nbelow seasonable levels today as northwest flow advects cooler air \nfrom Canada. Highs will be in the mid 60s to near 70 with clear \nskies. A few light breezes near the International Border, and \nChamplain Valley, to 10-15 MPH could bring a slight bite to the air \nFriday afternoon. Clear skies will persist through tonight with \nareawide strong radiational cooling. Temperatures will cool into the \nlow to mid 40s for most locations, and near 50 for areas around Lake \nChamplain. Raw guidance denotes low to mid 30s for areas in the \nheart of the Adirondacks. Tonight looks like the better night for \npatchy valley fog in the favored river valleys of Vermont and \nnorthern New York. Though it will not be widespread fog with it \nbeing 2 days detached from rain. Nonetheless, areas near Montpelier, \nthe lamoille Valley, CT river, and sheltered areas of the \nAdirondacks have a good chance for patchy fog. Saturday looks to be \na repeat of Friday with slightly warmer highs into the mid to upper \n40s with mostly clear skies. Gusty channeled winds in the Champlain \nValley, and mountain summit winds, will be possible by Saturday \nafternoon with winds turning more southerly, hence our warmer \ntemperature advection. Winds could gusts up to 25 MPH will be \npossible, with locally higher on Lake Champlain itself. A Lake Wind \nAdvisory may be needed during the day Saturday through Saturday \nevening. \n\nSaturday looks to remain dry up until the late evening. A weak \nshortwave looks to pass just to our south, brining some light shower \nchances to southern Vermont, mainly along or south of US-4. Showers \nwill be confined to the overnight hours, shifting east by sunrise. \nOvernight lows Saturday will be mild as a result into the upper 50s \nto low 60s. Clouds associated with the showers will build across the \nregion keeping the area socked in with cloud cover on Sunday. A \nfrontal passage will bring additional shower chances to the central \nand northern portions of the areas. Showers will be scattery and \nbroken in nature. Model soundings do show the potential for some \nisolated rumbles of thunder, mainly across northern New York, but \nany storms will be weak with limited instability, and lagging \ndynamics. Temperatures will return to seasonably cool level in the \n60s by Monday, with drying conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A fast/progressive flow aloft prevails acrs the northern \nCONUS for next week as our cwa remains in the battle ground btwn \nvery warm/moist airmass over the Ohio/Mississippi Valleys and much \ncooler/drier air to the north over central Canada. As boundary \nsharpens acrs the Mid Atlantic/NE CONUS for mid to late next week, \npotential for widespread precipitation increases for our region. \nStill a large spread in potential solutions from the globe guidance \nand associated ensemble members on placement and timing of heaviest \nprecip, given fast flow aloft and some agreement of boundary \nparalleling the mid/upper lvl winds. \n\nProgged 1025mb high pres directly overhead on Tues morning wl \nproduce light winds and clear skies with valley fog and pockets of \npatchy frost possible. Lows range from near freezing SLK/NEK to \nupper 40s near Lake Champlain. First weak boundary and associated \ns/w energy drops acrs our cwa late Tues into Weds with ribbon of \nenhanced mid lvl moisture, but limited instability. Expect a brief \nperiod of rain showers with latest WPC still indicating chc-likely \n(40 to 70%) pops. Also, progged 850mb winds of 30 to 40 knots are \nexpected acrs our cwa ahead of approaching boundary on Tues, so \nanticipated breezy south to southwest winds, especially \ncentral/northern CPV and higher trrn of dacks/northern Greens. Temps \nwl warm into the mid 70s to lower 80s per latest WPC fcst. Temps wl \nbe highly depend upon what side of the boundary our cwa wl be on for \nmid to late week, along with areas of clouds/precip. WPC has highs \nin the upper 60s to mid 70s with lows mid 40s to mid 50s for Weds \nthru Friday, which looks reasonable attm.",
